    <p class="caption">
      <b>Figure 1.</b> The agent loop and why a hit is cheap. Each turn re-sends the whole context; the
      engine keeps the previous turn's KV, so a turn whose prefix is still cached prefills only its new
      tokens. The workload is the paper's: tool times are exponential with mean 5 s, token ids are random
      and sessions share no prefixes.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container>
    <p class="text">
      The catch is who owns that cache. The cached KV belongs to sessions that sit idle in tool calls,
      and admitting waiting work evicts it. A burst can tip the engine into a trap: every turn misses,
      the queue grows, the gap between a session's turns grows with it, and the backlog does not drain,
      at a load the engine served before.
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      Quasi-static models read the trap as the falling branch of <Tex src={String.raw`\dot n=\lambda-\mu(n)`} />,
      with <Tex src={String.raw`\mu(n)`} /> the engine's throughput when <Tex src="n" /> sessions are kept
      running. On our engines, where every trapped turn misses, that reading gets the numbers wrong.
      Trapped runs complete sessions faster than the all-miss capacity, by up to 2.4 times (one
      from-empty run at 6.5 <Tex src={T.lref} />), and over the 57 stuck runs of the paired-test protocol
      their backlog grows, on average, at about two thirds of the quasi-static rate. An operator should
      care: in model F below, for this workload, a cut to just below the throughput measured in an
      all-miss trap does not clear it. We therefore ask two questions:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><p class="text">
        <b>What is the trapped state?</b> We idealize it as model F, a fluid FIFO server on which every
        turn misses, and derive its law exactly: along FIFO orbits its cycles obey a linear renewal
        recursion, and every trapped state converges to a known self-similar regime. In the model, for
        our workload, a trapped server completes sessions faster than its all-miss capacity: a youth
        premium.
      </p></li>
      <li><p class="text">
        <b>How does an engine get out?</b> We test two exits on real engines. Refusing arrivals drains
        the queue by shedding sessions; a session cap restores reuse, and rescued all 9 established traps
        it was tried on without turning sessions away.
      </p></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text">
      We claim none of the eviction, cold-prefill and queueing mechanisms that agent-serving systems
      describe; what we add is the law of the state they lead to, and a test of the ways out. Lu et al.
      (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.02543">arXiv:2611.02543</a>), who posted first, studied the
      same collapse concurrently and independently, on prefill–decode disaggregated SGLang. The table
      below collects our main results.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Result</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>200-session burst, no cap</td><td>engine at zero cache hits to the end in <b>30 of 30</b> runs</td><td>pre-registered (vLLM: registered)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Matched runs without the burst</td><td><b>every</b> run stayed good</td><td>same tests</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Client-side session cap from the start</td><td>trap prevented in <b>30 of 30</b> capped runs</td><td>same tests</td></tr>
          <tr><td>The registered trap rule</td><td>met at <b>9 of 10</b> rates (the tenth failed on backlog growth); it called 23 of the 30 BURST runs trapped</td><td>same tests</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Session cap engaged inside an established trap</td><td><b>9 of 9</b> rescued at the same load, no session shed</td><td>pre-registered</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Model F against every stuck run</td><td>served prompt within 3% in <b>50 of 57</b>; completions above the all-miss capacity in every one</td><td>retrospective, nothing fitted to trap data</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </Container>

  <!-- Model F -->
  <Container>
    <h1>Model F: A Renewal Law for the Trapped State</h1>
    <p class="text">
      To say what the trapped state is, we idealize it. We first give the intuition for why a trap
      holds, then the model and what it implies.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container>
    <h2>Why the Trap Holds</h2>
    <p class="text">
      Once the burst has passed, why does the engine not recover? Our answer here is a heuristic
      consistency argument, not part of the theorem. In a trap the KV pool turns over every
      <Tex src={String.raw`R_M=M/\mathrm{churn}\approx 26`} />–78 s (a turnover scale, not a pathwise
      eviction bound), while the gap between a session's turns keeps growing: in every BURST run of the
      first three registered tests the gap was 4.1–9.7 times <Tex src={T.RM} />, so an idle session's KV
      is overwritten long before it returns. In the good arms the same ratio is 0.010–0.028.
    </p>
  </Container>

