  <Container>
    <h2>The Youth Premium</h2>
    <p class="text">
      In model F the turns served are young, and in our workload young turns are cheap, so a trapped
      server completes sessions faster than its all-miss capacity: a youth premium. Theorem 2 splits it
      into a turn-count factor <Tex src="H" /> and a cost-growth factor <Tex src={String.raw`\Gamma`} />:
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`\frac{D}{\lambda_{\mathrm{miss}}}=H(z)\,\Gamma(z),\qquad H(z)=\frac{E[T]\,E[z^{-T}]}{\sum_kG_kz^{-k}},\qquad \Gamma(z)=\frac{E_\nu[c]}{E_{\nu^z}[c]}.`} />
    <p class="text">
      Near threshold, <Tex src={String.raw`D/\lambda_{\mathrm{miss}}-1=(\rho-1)\big(1-E[T]/\bar k_c\big)+O((\rho-1)^2)`} />.
      For our workload <Tex src={String.raw`E[T]/\bar k_c`} /> lies in 0.60–0.62, and the premium is
      positive at every load checked: proved by Theorem 2's monotonicity for
      <Tex src={String.raw`\rho\ge1.04`} />; below that it rests on a floating-point bracket and a
      first-order expansion. The premium is not automatic. With slowly growing costs
      <Tex src={String.raw`c_k=1+10^{-3}(k-1)`} /> it is negative at every load checked, and for an
      untruncated geometric <Tex src="T" /> with constant costs there is none.
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      This is what an operator needs. Corollary 3: after a switch to <Tex src={String.raw`\lambda'`} /> at
      <Tex src="t_0" />, the queue empties if and only if <Tex src={String.raw`\lambda'<\lambda_{\mathrm{miss}}`} />,
      after the busy period <Tex src={String.raw`V_{\mathrm{tot}}(t_0)/(1-\lambda'/\lambda_{\mathrm{miss}})`} />.
      The corollary is not new as mathematics, but with a premium it bites: a cut to any rate in
      <Tex src={String.raw`[\lambda_{\mathrm{miss}},D(\lambda))`} />, below the asymptotic trapped throughput,
      does not empty model F's queue. In the model, a trap's measured throughput overstates the recovery
      threshold. Remark 1 adds that <Tex src="z" /> solves <Tex src={String.raw`\rho\sum_k\omega_kz^{-k}=1`} />
      with <Tex src={String.raw`\omega_k=c_kG_k/\sum_jc_jG_j`} />, so the trapped composition and
      <Tex src={String.raw`D/\lambda_{\mathrm{miss}}`} /> are unchanged by a uniform GPU speed-up at equal
      <Tex src={String.raw`\rho`} />; this is not a general hardware claim.
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      The costs come from the paper's calibrated prefill model,
      <Tex src={String.raw`t_P(x,y)=a\max(1,x/B_c)+\max(f_0,bx)+c_p\,x\,\big(y+(x+1)/2\big)`} />, with
      <Tex src={String.raw`a=9.24`} /> ms, <Tex src={String.raw`f_0=1.26`} /> ms,
      <Tex src={String.raw`b=62.6\,\mu`} />s/token, <Tex src={String.raw`c_p=2.24`} /> ns per causal pair and
      chunk <Tex src={String.raw`B_c=8192`} />. Then <Tex src={String.raw`c_k=t^m_k+\delta`} />, the miss
      prefill plus <Tex src={String.raw`\delta`} />, the all-miss per-turn decode share, chosen so that
      <Tex src={T.lmiss} /> matches the first-principles model's capacity. The calculator below evaluates
      these statements in your browser.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container width="extra-large" tone="gray">
    <TrapPerronCalculator />
    <p class="caption">
      <b>Figure 5.</b> Model F, computed in your browser. For the chosen workload and load the calculator
      solves the Perron equation by bisection and evaluates Theorem 1(a) and Theorem 2. Left, the turns a
      trapped server serves (bars) against the stationary mix (line); right, <Tex src={String.raw`D/\lambda_{\mathrm{miss}}`} />
      across loads. The paper does not print <Tex src={String.raw`\delta`} />: for the standard workload the
      site's value gives <Tex src={String.raw`\lambda_{\mathrm{miss}}=1.004\,\lambda_{\mathrm{ref}}`} />, and
      for MIRROR it is pinned to the stated <Tex src={String.raw`1.046\,\lambda_{\mathrm{ref}}`} />. Beside
      each reading is the paper's published model-F value for the 300k cell, or MIRROR's frozen prediction,
      where one exists; agreement means within one unit of the last printed digit. These are model F's
      asymptotic values: in the registered window, a transient of order 10% remains. The calculator
      shows model values only; the comparisons with the engine in the next sections are retrospective
      except where marked blind.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <!-- Experiments -->
  <Container>
    <h1>Experiments</h1>
    <p class="text">
      The registered tests on real engines come first, then the theory against every stuck run, a
      blind test, and the two ways out.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container>
    <h2>Setup and Baselines</h2>
    <p class="text">
      The registered tests drive real engines on Iowa State's Nova cluster, via Slurm, with one NVIDIA
      A100 80GB PCIe or H200 NVL per engine, under a synthetic agent workload. A session has
      <Tex src="T" /> turns, and turn <Tex src="k" /> sends a prompt of <Tex src="L_k" /> tokens:
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`T=\min(\mathrm{Geometric}(1/15),60),\qquad L_k=2000+650(k-1)\ \text{tokens},`} />
    <p class="text">
      so <Tex src={String.raw`E[T]=14.76`} />. The engines are SGLang 0.5.22 and vLLM 0.32.0, serving
      Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct or Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, with the KV pool capped at 300k tokens (150k or 600k in
      the pool sweep; SGLang's native 955,757 tokens in the native cell). Rates are multiples of each
      cell's reference rate <Tex src={T.lref} />, the all-miss session rate measured with prefix caching
      disabled.
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      The main protocol, E2, runs three arms per seed on common arrivals: BURST (200 sessions within 60 s
      at 1200 s), NOBURST, and CAPPED, with a client-side FIFO session cap from the start. Runs last 8700 s
      and are judged over <Tex src={String.raw`[5700,8700)`} /> s. BURST counts as trapped if its uncached
      prefill token rate is within ±15% of the all-miss rate and its backlog slope is above
      <Tex src={String.raw`0.5(\lambda-\lambda_{\mathrm{ref}})`} />. Each test's rule was committed to git
      before its jobs were submitted; there is no third-party registry. <em>Pre-registered</em> means the
      rule was also pushed to GitHub before submission; <em>registered</em> means it was committed before
      and pushed later.
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      The quasi-static baseline rests on the engine's closed-population throughput curve
      <Tex src={String.raw`\mu(n)`} />, the completion rate with <Tex src="n" /> sessions kept running,
      which has a cliff. The 300k-token pool limit did not create that cliff; it moved it. On an H200 at
      the 300k pool the window between the all-miss capacity and the curve's peak narrows: the all-miss
      capacity rose 2.71-fold and the peak of
      <Tex src={String.raw`\mu(n)`} /> only 1.63-fold. A first-principles model of the curve put its 0.5-hit
