  <Container>
    <h2>Blind Tests</h2>
    <p class="text">
      We then tested the theory blind in outcome: the predictions were frozen before the runs they score
      existed. Each test carries its status from the paper's evidence ledger, with its caveats.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><p class="text">
        <b>Tool-slowdown traps</b> (pre-registered, blind in outcome; tolerances set on earlier runs).
        Across the four blind runs trapped by a tool slowdown alone, pre-set scores S1–S4 pass in both
        cells and S5 is close in 3 of 4. The per-run slope check failed in 2 of 4 runs.
      </p></li>
      <li><p class="text">
        <b>Which seeds a slowdown traps</b> (pre-registered, two frozen predictors). Outcome: NO-TRAP, as
        the simulator said, seed by seed; the first-principles fluid verdict, TRAPS, missed.
      </p></li>
      <li><p class="text">
        <b>Cut traps, CUT-A and CUT-B</b> (pre-registered, blind in outcome; the rule was fixed after the
        predictions were read). Cutting arrivals into <Tex src={String.raw`[\lambda_{\mathrm{miss}},D)`} />:
        all three CUT-A runs stayed stuck to the end at zero hits, and all three seeds were
        theory-favoured. Cutting below capacity: all three CUT-B runs were still stuck at 8700 s, with 140,
        77 and 89 sessions present against model F's 139, 79 and 94. Intermediate readings fit as well;
        what singles out model F against them is post hoc.
      </p></li>
    </ul>
  </Container>

  <NoteRow>
    <p class="text">
      <b>MIRROR</b> (pre-registered, blind in outcome; the workload was chosen with the model). Theorem 2
      says the premium can reverse. With a 20-step budget, <Tex src={String.raw`T=\min(\mathrm{Geometric}(1/1000),20)`} />,
      model F's asymptotic <Tex src={String.raw`D/\lambda_{\mathrm{miss}}`} /> at 1.65 <Tex src={T.lref} /> is
      0.771 (<Tex src="H=0.699" />, <Tex src={String.raw`\Gamma=1.103`} />), below 1. Outcome: PASS at both
      rates, RENEWAL in 3 of 3 seeds each. In the last part of each run, the MIRROR runs at
      1.65 <Tex src={T.lref} /> completed 0.50–0.61 <Tex src={T.lref} />, below the measured capacity of
      1.06 <Tex src={T.lref} /> (Figure 9a). That is a transient lull: MIRROR measures it, not the
      asymptotic <Tex src="D" /> (0.81 <Tex src={T.lref} /> in model F), and rejects only the
      throughput-curve readings.
    </p>
    <Fragment slot="note">
      Model F's dashed lines in Figure 9 were frozen under tag <code>r16-mirror-reg</code> before any
      MIRROR job. Select MIRROR in the calculator above to recompute 0.771 (it gives 0.7705, within the
      rounding of the stated capacity 1.046 <Tex src={T.lref} />).
    </Fragment>
  </NoteRow>

