  <Container>
    <h2>Two Ways Out</h2>
    <p class="text">
      In model F, Corollary 3 says a cut in arrivals must land below <Tex src={T.lmiss} /> to drain the
      queue. We illustrate it in the all-miss fluid for the 300k cell at 1.65 <Tex src={T.lref} />, where
      <Tex src={String.raw`D=1.223\,\lambda_{\mathrm{miss}}`} />, cutting at 5000 s with a backlog of 251.
      This is model output, not engine data.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Cut to</th><th>Backlog after the cut (model F)</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><Tex src={String.raw`1.18\,\lambda_{\mathrm{miss}}`} /></td><td>dips to 250.7 (−0.3%), then grows to 445 by 30,000 s</td></tr>
          <tr><td><Tex src={String.raw`1.10\,\lambda_{\mathrm{miss}}`} /></td><td>dips to 246 (−2%), then grows to 352</td></tr>
          <tr><td><Tex src={String.raw`1.05\,\lambda_{\mathrm{miss}}`} /></td><td>dips to 241 (−4%), then grows to 295</td></tr>
          <tr><td><Tex src={String.raw`1.02\,\lambda_{\mathrm{miss}}`} /></td><td>dips to 237.6 (−5.5%), then grows to 261</td></tr>
          <tr><td><Tex src={String.raw`1.00\,\lambda_{\mathrm{miss}}`} /></td><td>undershoots to 234.6 (−6.7%), settles at 238.7</td></tr>
          <tr><td><Tex src={String.raw`0.90\,\lambda_{\mathrm{miss}}`} /></td><td>drains slowly; empty at 59,655 s, the busy period <Tex src={String.raw`V_{\mathrm{tot}}/(1-\rho')`} /></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="caption">
      <b>Table 3.</b> Corollary 3 illustrated (paper App. A). Every cut at or above
      <Tex src={T.lmiss} /> leaves the trap growing or level; only a cut below it drains, after a busy
      period that diverges as the cut approaches <Tex src={T.lmiss} />.
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      On the engine we tested two exits. Refusing arrivals works by shedding: a 600 s stop refused 56–59
      sessions and left both runs stuck; a 3600 s stop refused 355–378 and left both good. A session cap
      restores reuse instead. Engaged inside an established trap, it holds the admitted sessions below
      the all-miss onset <Tex src={T.nb} />, so their turns hit again, and the excess waits at the client.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <NoteRow>
    <p class="text">
      In the registered rescue tests R1 and R4, all 9 runs were rescued without shedding a session. With
      the cap removed, every run stayed good at the same arrival process for the remaining 2560–4720 s.
      The price is waiting at the client: 386–569 s per paused session on average. One frozen prediction
      missed: the first-principles model predicted the rescue in every cell, but 4 of its 9 release times
      fell outside its ±15% tolerance.
    </p>
    <Fragment slot="note">
      The cap comes off once both client queues have been empty for 300 s. Rows of Table 4 below the
      release row are descriptive, computed after the registered verdict.
    </Fragment>
