  <!-- Conclusions -->
  <Container>
    <h1>Conclusions and Limitations</h1>
    <p class="text">
      A burst of agent sessions can leave a prefix-cached LLM serving engine missing every turn, at a
      load it served before. In registered tests on two engines,
      two GPUs and two models, a 200-session burst left the engine at zero hits to the end in all 30
      runs. For an idealization of the trapped state, model F, we proved that the cycles along FIFO
      orbits obey a linear renewal recursion whose Perron root fixes the dynamics: every trapped state
      converges to a self-similar regime in which the turns served are young and short. For our workload
      this gives a youth premium, so in the model a trap's measured throughput overstates the recovery
      threshold. Of the two exits, refusing arrivals sheds sessions, while a session cap rescued 9 of 9
      established traps at the same load without shedding, and the engine stayed good after release.
    </p>
    <p class="text">Limitations:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><p class="text">
        Model F is an idealization, and the paper does not prove that the engine converges to it. It
        leaves out concurrency, preemption and the transitions into and out of the trap. In the
        registered window, about 33–37 generations after the burst, a transient of order 10% remains for
        this workload.
      </p></li>
      <li><p class="text">
        The premium is proved positive for <Tex src={String.raw`\rho\ge1.04`} />; below that it rests on a
        floating-point bracket and a first-order expansion. The repository's theory tests check most
        statements numerically, but not the exact identity of Lemma 1, Theorem 1(b), Remark 1, Corollary 3
        beyond the subcritical busy period, or the monotonicity statements of Theorem 2.
      </p></li>
      <li><p class="text">
        Why the trap persists, the gap outrunning the pool turnover, is a heuristic consistency argument,
        not part of the theorem. "Stays stuck" always means within the run's horizon.
      </p></li>
      <li><p class="text">
        The match with the 57 stuck runs is retrospective. Among the blind tests, the CUT rule was fixed
        after the predictions were read, the tool-slowdown per-run slope check failed in 2 of 4 runs, and
        MIRROR's workload was chosen with the model.
      </p></li>
      <li><p class="text">
        One registered trap rule failed (Llama-3.1-8B at the lower rate), as did the first tool-slowdown
        rule. The tests use a synthetic workload with random token ids and no shared prefixes (MIRROR caps
        its steps), on two engines, two GPUs and two models.
      </p></li>
    </ul>
  </Container>

